1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,947
The simple tests can show that a grammar is not L L one. This video gives the full test. It proves that a grammar is L L one, or shows exactly where it fails.

2
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:35,863
We compute four things: which non-terminals are nullable, the first sets, the follow sets, and the predict sets. We do it for the F grammar from the notes. Then we solve three exercises from the notes. We use the names of the week 6 slides: E P S, First, Follow and Predict. Videos 19 c and 19 d work the slides' own examples.

3
00:00:36,313 --> 00:00:45,124
Take FactorTail from the B N F grammar of the last video. It has two productions: dot Factor FactorTail, and epsilon.

4
00:00:45,574 --> 00:00:52,977
The first production starts with a dot. So the lookahead dot chooses it. Its predict set is the set with the dot.

5
00:00:53,427 --> 00:01:01,619
Which tokens choose the epsilon production? Nothing in the production tells us. It depends on what can come after FactorTail.

6
00:01:02,069 --> 00:01:06,719
The predict set of a production is the set of lookahead tokens that choose it.

7
00:01:07,169 --> 00:01:13,420
If the predict sets of each non-terminal's productions are disjoint, the grammar is L L one.

8
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:25,134
The slides give a recipe in five steps. Step 1: convert the grammar from E B N F to B N F. Video 19 a did that for the F grammar.

9
00:01:25,584 --> 00:01:32,389
Step 2: nullable. Nullable means that the non-terminal can derive epsilon, the empty string.

10
00:01:32,839 --> 00:01:35,527
Step 3, the first sets, needs nullable.

11
00:01:35,977 --> 00:01:39,497
Step 4, the follow sets, needs nullable and first.

12
00:01:39,947 --> 00:01:43,019
Step 5, the predict sets, needs all three.

13
00:01:43,469 --> 00:01:54,349
The method is the same as in your other engineering courses. Each step is a system of equations. Here the variables are sets, and the operation is union.

14
00:01:54,799 --> 00:02:03,951
We solve by iteration. Start with empty sets, and apply all equations. Repeat until nothing changes. That is a fixed point.

15
00:02:04,401 --> 00:02:10,908
Here again is the F grammar in B N F, from the last video. It has 17 productions.

16
00:02:11,358 --> 00:02:15,582
The non-terminals start with a capital letter. There are 10 of them.

17
00:02:16,032 --> 00:02:25,867
There are 11 terminals: the arrow, semicolon, plus, dot, not, the two parentheses, 0, 1, i d, and the end marker, dollar dollar.

18
00:02:26,317 --> 00:02:36,962
Nullable means that the non-terminal can derive epsilon. Then it can vanish. The slides write E P S of alpha, with the answer yes or no.

19
00:02:37,412 --> 00:02:43,897
The notes write eps of A equals 1 or 0. It is the same idea. We use the slides' form.

20
00:02:44,347 --> 00:02:53,670
Case 1: if the non-terminal derives epsilon directly, it is nullable. FList goes to epsilon, so FList is nullable.

21
00:02:54,120 --> 00:03:05,085
Case 2: a production that contains a terminal cannot vanish. A terminal is a token, and no rule can erase it. Here the arrow and the semicolon stay.

22
00:03:05,535 --> 00:03:15,327
Case 3: a production with only non-terminals vanishes if every symbol in it is nullable. Here Term and TermTail must both be nullable.

23
00:03:15,777 --> 00:03:20,663
So X is nullable if at least one of its productions can vanish completely.

24
00:03:21,113 --> 00:03:31,289
The notes write this as a sum of products, one product for each production. For example, eps of Expr is eps of Term times eps of TermTail.

25
00:03:31,739 --> 00:03:39,995
Now fill the nullable table for the F grammar. The grammar is on the left. The table has one row for each non-terminal.

26
00:03:40,445 --> 00:03:44,839
FList goes to epsilon directly. So FList is nullable.

27
00:03:45,289 --> 00:03:49,727
TermTail goes to epsilon directly. So TermTail is nullable.

28
00:03:50,177 --> 00:03:54,849
FactorTail goes to epsilon directly. So FactorTail is nullable.

29
00:03:55,299 --> 00:04:03,277
These three non-terminals are new in video 19 a. Each one replaced a star of the E B N F grammar.

30
00:04:03,727 --> 00:04:12,133
Program goes to FList dollar dollar. It contains the terminal dollar dollar, which never vanishes. So Program: no.

31
00:04:12,583 --> 00:04:17,553
Formula contains the arrow and the semicolon. So Formula: no.

32
00:04:18,003 --> 00:04:23,081
Constant goes to 0 or to 1. Both are terminals. So Constant: no.

33
00:04:23,531 --> 00:04:26,731
Var goes to i d, a terminal. So Var: no.

34
00:04:27,181 --> 00:04:36,247
Factor has four productions. Not Factor, and open parenthesis Expr close parenthesis, contain terminals. They cannot vanish.

35
00:04:36,697 --> 00:04:45,060
The other two are Factor goes to Var, and Factor goes to Constant. Var and Constant are not nullable. So Factor: no.

36
00:04:45,510 --> 00:04:50,609
Term goes to Factor FactorTail. Factor is not nullable, so Term: no.

37
00:04:51,059 --> 00:04:56,115
Expr goes to Term TermTail. Term is not nullable, so Expr: no.

38
00:04:56,565 --> 00:05:05,930
So three non-terminals are nullable: FList, TermTail and FactorTail. Our fixed point routine in python gives the same three.

39
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:15,873
The first set of a string of symbols is the set of terminals that can be leftmost in a string derived from it. You do not have to finish the derivation.

40
00:05:16,323 --> 00:05:23,150
To compute it, read the right side from left to right. Take Program, which goes to FList dollar dollar.

41
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:38,640
Add the first set of the first symbol, FList. FList derives Formula FList, then Var arrow Expr semicolon FList, then a string that starts with i d. So first of FList is the set i d.

42
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:45,639
If that symbol is nullable, it can vanish. So also look at the next symbol. FList is nullable.

43
00:05:46,089 --> 00:05:50,143
The next symbol is dollar dollar, a terminal. Add it and stop.

44
00:05:50,593 --> 00:05:53,921
So first of Program is i d and dollar dollar.

45
00:05:54,371 --> 00:06:02,285
First sets never contain epsilon. First of epsilon is the empty set, because epsilon is not a token.

46
00:06:02,735 --> 00:06:10,309
Dollar dollar is a real token, so it can be in a first set. Nullability is the separate E P S question.

47
00:06:10,759 --> 00:06:17,628
Now fill the first table for the F grammar. The nullable non-terminals, from the last table, are above it.

48
00:06:18,078 --> 00:06:21,918
Var goes to i d. So first of Var is i d.

49
00:06:22,368 --> 00:06:27,659
Constant goes to 0 or to 1. So first of Constant is 0 and 1.

50
00:06:28,109 --> 00:06:39,650
TermTail goes to plus Term TermTail, or to epsilon. The first production starts with plus. The epsilon production adds nothing. So first of TermTail is plus.

51
00:06:40,100 --> 00:06:47,247
FactorTail goes to dot Factor FactorTail, or to epsilon. So first of FactorTail is the dot.

52
00:06:47,697 --> 00:06:54,054
Factor has four productions. Two of them start with a terminal: not, and open parenthesis.

53
00:06:54,504 --> 00:07:02,461
The other two start with Var and with Constant. Add first of Var, i d, and first of Constant, 0 and 1.

54
00:07:02,911 --> 00:07:07,967
So first of Factor is not, open parenthesis, 0, 1 and i d.

55
00:07:08,417 --> 00:07:16,545
Term goes to Factor FactorTail. Factor is not nullable, so only Factor counts. First of Term is first of Factor.

56
00:07:16,995 --> 00:07:23,545
Expr goes to Term TermTail. Term is not nullable. So first of Expr is first of Term.

57
00:07:23,995 --> 00:07:29,925
Formula starts with Var. So first of Formula is first of Var, which is i d.

58
00:07:30,375 --> 00:07:41,810
FList goes to Formula FList. Formula is not nullable, so first of FList is first of Formula, i d. The epsilon production adds nothing.

59
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:52,009
Program goes to FList dollar dollar. FList is nullable, so also add dollar dollar. First of Program is i d and dollar dollar.

60
00:07:52,459 --> 00:07:56,257
All ten values match the notes and our python routine.

61
00:07:56,707 --> 00:08:07,971
The follow set of A is the set of terminals that can come right after A, in some string derived from the start symbol. Follow is defined only for non-terminals.

62
00:08:08,421 --> 00:08:12,943
You do not look inside A. You look at the right sides where A is used.

63
00:08:13,393 --> 00:08:25,809
Case 1: something, beta, comes after A in a production for D. Add first of beta. For example, in Formula, the arrow comes after Var. So the arrow is in follow of Var.

64
00:08:26,259 --> 00:08:45,097
Case 2: A is at the end of a production for D, or everything after A is nullable. Then whatever follows D can follow A. Add follow of D. For example, TermTail is at the end of Expr goes to Term TermTail. So follow of Expr goes into follow of TermTail.

65
00:08:45,547 --> 00:08:54,421
When D is A itself, this adds nothing new. The notes skip that case. TermTail at the end of its own production is an example.

66
00:08:54,871 --> 00:08:57,410
Union the results from every use of A.

67
00:08:57,860 --> 00:09:07,460
The start symbol, Program, is never on a right side. So its follow set is empty. The grammar writes the end marker, dollar dollar, itself.

68
00:09:07,910 --> 00:09:12,326
Program is never on a right side. So its follow set is empty.

69
00:09:12,776 --> 00:09:18,515
FList is used in Program goes to FList dollar dollar. Dollar dollar follows it.

70
00:09:18,965 --> 00:09:30,549
FList is also at the end of its own production, FList goes to Formula FList. Here D is A, so it adds nothing. Follow of FList is dollar dollar.

71
00:09:30,999 --> 00:09:40,001
Formula is used in FList goes to Formula FList. FList comes after it. Add first of FList, which is i d.

72
00:09:40,451 --> 00:09:48,985
FList is nullable, so also add follow of FList, dollar dollar. Follow of Formula is i d and dollar dollar.

73
00:09:49,435 --> 00:09:56,795
Expr is used twice. In Formula goes to Var arrow Expr semicolon, a semicolon follows it.

74
00:09:57,245 --> 00:10:08,061
In Factor goes to open parenthesis Expr close parenthesis, a close parenthesis follows it. So follow of Expr is close parenthesis and semicolon.

75
00:10:08,511 --> 00:10:16,809
TermTail is at the end of Expr goes to Term TermTail. So it gets follow of Expr: close parenthesis and semicolon.

76
00:10:17,259 --> 00:10:26,091
TermTail is also at the end of its own production, TermTail goes to plus Term TermTail. Here D is A, so it adds nothing.

77
00:10:26,541 --> 00:10:37,976
Term is used in Expr goes to Term TermTail, and in TermTail goes to plus Term TermTail. TermTail follows it in both. Add first of TermTail, the plus.

78
00:10:38,426 --> 00:10:50,906
TermTail is nullable. So add follow of Expr. The second use adds follow of TermTail, which is the same set. Follow of Term is plus, close parenthesis and semicolon.

79
00:10:51,356 --> 00:10:59,548
FactorTail is at the end of Term goes to Factor FactorTail. So it gets follow of Term. Its own production adds nothing.

80
00:10:59,998 --> 00:11:07,315
Factor is followed by FactorTail, in Term goes to Factor FactorTail. Add first of FactorTail, the dot.

81
00:11:07,765 --> 00:11:16,213
FactorTail is nullable, so add follow of Term. Follow of Factor is dot, plus, close parenthesis and semicolon.

82
00:11:16,663 --> 00:11:25,581
Factor is also used in FactorTail goes to dot Factor FactorTail, and at the end of not Factor. These uses add nothing new.

83
00:11:26,031 --> 00:11:32,495
Constant is at the end of Factor goes to Constant. So follow of Constant is follow of Factor.

84
00:11:32,945 --> 00:11:37,638
Var is used in two right sides. In Formula, the arrow follows it.

85
00:11:38,088 --> 00:11:48,861
In Factor goes to Var, Var is at the end. So add follow of Factor. Follow of Var is the arrow, dot, plus, close parenthesis and semicolon.

86
00:11:49,311 --> 00:11:53,365
All ten follow sets match the notes and our python routine.

87
00:11:53,815 --> 00:11:58,913
Now the predict set of a production, A goes to alpha. It is first of alpha.

88
00:11:59,363 --> 00:12:04,931
If alpha can vanish, add follow of A too. Here alpha is the whole right side.

89
00:12:05,381 --> 00:12:12,848
Back to FactorTail. For dot Factor FactorTail, alpha starts with a dot. The predict set is just the dot.

90
00:12:13,298 --> 00:12:19,719
For FactorTail goes to epsilon, first of epsilon is empty, and epsilon vanishes.

91
00:12:20,169 --> 00:12:27,572
So the predict set is follow of FactorTail, from the follow table: plus, close parenthesis and semicolon.

92
00:12:28,022 --> 00:12:34,955
The two sets are disjoint. So one token of lookahead always chooses the right FactorTail production.

93
00:12:35,405 --> 00:12:44,728
Now the predict set of every production. Program, Formula, Expr and Term have only one production each. So they cannot conflict.

94
00:12:45,178 --> 00:12:52,175
Their predict sets come from first sets. Program also adds dollar dollar, because FList is nullable.

95
00:12:52,625 --> 00:13:00,647
FList has two productions. FList goes to Formula FList is predicted by first of Formula, i d.

96
00:13:01,097 --> 00:13:06,387
FList goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of FList, dollar dollar.

97
00:13:06,837 --> 00:13:09,845
i d against dollar dollar: disjoint.

98
00:13:10,295 --> 00:13:14,413
TermTail goes to plus Term TermTail is predicted by plus.

99
00:13:14,863 --> 00:13:23,567
TermTail goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of TermTail: close parenthesis and semicolon. Disjoint from plus.

100
00:13:24,017 --> 00:13:28,689
FactorTail goes to dot Factor FactorTail is predicted by the dot.

101
00:13:29,139 --> 00:13:38,803
FactorTail goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of FactorTail: plus, close parenthesis and semicolon. Disjoint from the dot.

102
00:13:39,253 --> 00:13:44,757
The rest of the productions. Var has one production. It is predicted by i d.

103
00:13:45,207 --> 00:13:55,596
Factor has four productions. Not Factor is predicted by not. Open parenthesis Expr close parenthesis is predicted by the open parenthesis.

104
00:13:56,046 --> 00:14:05,753
Factor goes to Var is predicted by first of Var, i d. Factor goes to Constant is predicted by first of Constant, 0 and 1.

105
00:14:06,203 --> 00:14:10,768
No token appears twice in the four Factor sets. They are disjoint.

106
00:14:11,218 --> 00:14:14,183
Constant: 0 against 1. Disjoint.

107
00:14:14,633 --> 00:14:22,015
For every non-terminal, the predict sets of its productions are disjoint. So the F grammar is L L one.

108
00:14:22,465 --> 00:14:29,441
These predict sets, and the ones on the last slide, match the notes. Our python check finds no overlap.

109
00:14:29,891 --> 00:14:43,331
Here is the parser at work on the input i d, arrow, 0, semicolon, dollar dollar. Each row shows the lookahead token, the production it chooses, and the predict set of that production.

110
00:14:43,781 --> 00:14:49,733
Lookahead i d. Program has one production. Its predict set contains i d.

111
00:14:50,183 --> 00:14:54,663
i d is in the predict set of FList goes to Formula FList.

112
00:14:55,113 --> 00:14:59,230
Formula has one production. i d is in its predict set.

113
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:05,355
Var goes to i d. The parser matches i d, and then the arrow, with the input.

114
00:15:05,805 --> 00:15:11,821
i d and the arrow are read. The lookahead is now 0. Expr has one production.

115
00:15:12,271 --> 00:15:14,041
Term has one production.

116
00:15:14,491 --> 00:15:18,203
0 is in the predict set of Factor goes to Constant.

117
00:15:18,653 --> 00:15:23,560
And in the predict set of Constant goes to 0. The parser matches 0.

118
00:15:24,010 --> 00:15:31,498
The lookahead is now the semicolon. It is in the follow set of FactorTail, so FactorTail takes epsilon.

119
00:15:31,948 --> 00:15:40,289
The semicolon is also in the follow set of TermTail. TermTail takes epsilon. The parser matches the semicolon.

120
00:15:40,739 --> 00:15:48,099
The lookahead dollar dollar is in the predict set of FList goes to epsilon. The parser matches dollar dollar.

121
00:15:48,549 --> 00:15:58,704
The parser accepts. Every choice came from one token of lookahead. The epsilon choices, in purple, are the ones that needed the follow sets.

122
00:15:59,154 --> 00:16:11,207
Now an exercise from the notes. S is the start symbol. a, b, c, d, f and g are terminals. Compute the first and follow sets of each non-terminal.

123
00:16:11,657 --> 00:16:13,300
Pause the video and try it.

124
00:16:13,750 --> 00:16:21,131
First, nullable. S goes to X Y Z dollar dollar. It contains dollar dollar, so S is not nullable.

125
00:16:21,581 --> 00:16:25,421
X goes to epsilon directly. So X is nullable.

126
00:16:25,871 --> 00:16:29,797
Y goes to epsilon directly. So Y is nullable.

127
00:16:30,247 --> 00:16:35,431
Z goes to f or to g. Both are terminals, so Z is not nullable.

128
00:16:35,881 --> 00:16:45,033
Now the first sets. Y starts with c or with d. Its epsilon production adds nothing. So first of Y is c and d.

129
00:16:45,483 --> 00:16:50,197
Z starts with f or with g. So first of Z is f and g.

130
00:16:50,647 --> 00:16:58,029
X starts with a, or with Z. First of Z is f and g. So first of X is a, f and g.

131
00:16:58,479 --> 00:17:02,895
S goes to X Y Z dollar dollar. Start with first of X.

132
00:17:03,345 --> 00:17:06,758
X is nullable, so also add first of Y.

133
00:17:07,208 --> 00:17:12,499
Y is nullable too, so add first of Z. Z is not nullable, so stop.

134
00:17:12,949 --> 00:17:17,983
First of S is a, c, d, f and g. All values match the notes.

135
00:17:18,433 --> 00:17:24,023
Now the follow sets. S is never on a right side. Its follow set is empty.

136
00:17:24,473 --> 00:17:35,267
Start with Y. In S goes to X Y Z dollar dollar, Z follows Y. Z is not nullable. So follow of Y is first of Z: f and g.

137
00:17:35,717 --> 00:17:42,331
The Y at the end of d X Y is in Y's own production. Here D is A, so it adds nothing.

138
00:17:42,781 --> 00:17:51,869
X is used twice. In S, Y Z dollar dollar follows it. Add first of Y. Y is nullable, so add first of Z too.

139
00:17:52,319 --> 00:18:00,212
In Y goes to d X Y, Y follows X. Add first of Y. Y is nullable, so add follow of Y.

140
00:18:00,662 --> 00:18:10,518
Follow of Y equals first of Z. So the notes write first of Y union follow of Y. Follow of X is c, d, f and g.

141
00:18:10,968 --> 00:18:16,088
Z is followed by dollar dollar in S goes to X Y Z dollar dollar.

142
00:18:16,538 --> 00:18:23,215
And Z is followed by b in X goes to Z b. So follow of Z is b and dollar dollar.

143
00:18:23,665 --> 00:18:27,121
All values match the notes and our python routine.

144
00:18:27,571 --> 00:18:35,337
One more check that the question did not ask for. X goes to Z b is predicted by first of Z: f and g.

145
00:18:35,787 --> 00:18:41,632
X goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of X: c, d, f and g.

146
00:18:42,082 --> 00:18:46,797
Both sets contain f and g. So this grammar is not L L one.

147
00:18:47,247 --> 00:18:58,831
The last full exercise. Is this grammar L L one? Prove it with the four kinds of sets. Give the equations and the solutions. The grammar is in E B N F.

148
00:18:59,281 --> 00:19:00,923
Pause the video and try it.

149
00:19:01,373 --> 00:19:05,917
First convert to B N F. The bar in S gives two productions.

150
00:19:06,367 --> 00:19:13,343
The notes call the start symbol G. So G goes to A B dollar dollar, or to C x dollar dollar.

151
00:19:13,793 --> 00:19:19,873
The star in A becomes recursion with epsilon: A goes to y A, or to epsilon.

152
00:19:20,323 --> 00:19:23,161
B goes to C q. It does not change.

153
00:19:23,611 --> 00:19:28,475
C goes to p C, or to epsilon. The bar gives two productions.

154
00:19:28,925 --> 00:19:31,741
The B N F grammar has seven productions.

155
00:19:32,191 --> 00:19:38,249
Nullable first. Both G productions contain dollar dollar. So G is not nullable.

156
00:19:38,699 --> 00:19:42,091
A goes to epsilon directly. So A is nullable.

157
00:19:42,541 --> 00:19:47,064
B goes to C q, and q is a terminal. So B is not nullable.

158
00:19:47,514 --> 00:19:51,269
C goes to epsilon directly. So C is nullable.

159
00:19:51,719 --> 00:19:55,601
Now first. A goes to y A, so first of A is y.

160
00:19:56,051 --> 00:19:59,230
C goes to p C, so first of C is p.

161
00:19:59,680 --> 00:20:02,944
B goes to C q. Add first of C, the p.

162
00:20:03,394 --> 00:20:08,023
C is nullable, so also add q. First of B is p and q.

163
00:20:08,473 --> 00:20:16,751
G goes to A B dollar dollar. Add first of A. A is nullable, so add first of B. B is not nullable, so stop.

164
00:20:17,201 --> 00:20:23,131
G goes to C x dollar dollar. Add first of C. C is nullable, so add x.

165
00:20:23,581 --> 00:20:29,043
Together, first of G is p, q, x and y. All values match the notes.

166
00:20:29,493 --> 00:20:34,463
Now follow. G is never on a right side, so its follow set is empty.

167
00:20:34,913 --> 00:20:43,660
A is followed by B, in G goes to A B dollar dollar. B is not nullable. So follow of A is first of B: p and q.

168
00:20:44,110 --> 00:20:48,547
A at the end of y A is in A's own production. It adds nothing.

169
00:20:48,997 --> 00:20:53,520
B is followed by dollar dollar. So follow of B is dollar dollar.

170
00:20:53,970 --> 00:20:58,365
C is followed by x, in G goes to C x dollar dollar.

171
00:20:58,815 --> 00:21:02,612
And C is followed by q, in B goes to C q.

172
00:21:03,062 --> 00:21:10,422
C at the end of p C is in C's own production. It adds nothing. So follow of C is q and x.

173
00:21:10,872 --> 00:21:14,627
Now predict. A goes to y A is predicted by y.

174
00:21:15,077 --> 00:21:20,965
A goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of A: p and q. Disjoint from y.

175
00:21:21,415 --> 00:21:29,585
B has one production. B goes to C q is predicted by first of C, the p, and q, because C is nullable.

176
00:21:30,035 --> 00:21:39,166
C goes to p C is predicted by p. C goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of C: q and x. Disjoint.

177
00:21:39,616 --> 00:21:46,400
G goes to A B dollar dollar is predicted by first of A and first of B: p, q and y.

178
00:21:46,850 --> 00:21:52,589
G goes to C x dollar dollar is predicted by first of C and x: p and x.

179
00:21:53,039 --> 00:21:59,097
Both G sets contain p. They are not disjoint. So the grammar is not L L one.

180
00:21:59,547 --> 00:22:05,798
Here is why, with two inputs. The input p q needs G goes to A B dollar dollar.

181
00:22:06,248 --> 00:22:12,456
A derives epsilon. B goes to C q. C goes to p C, and then to epsilon.

182
00:22:12,906 --> 00:22:16,767
The input p x needs G goes to C x dollar dollar.

183
00:22:17,217 --> 00:22:20,311
C goes to p C, and then to epsilon.

184
00:22:20,761 --> 00:22:26,990
Both inputs start with p. With one token of lookahead, the parser sees only the p.

185
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:32,837
It must look two tokens ahead, to the q or the x, to choose the G production.

186
00:22:33,287 --> 00:22:39,922
So the answer is no, as the notes say. A python check gives the same single overlap, on p.

187
00:22:40,372 --> 00:22:54,089
One more grammar from the notes, Exercise 4.11.7. It has normal arithmetic precedence and right associativity. The notes say that it is also L L one. Check it.

188
00:22:54,539 --> 00:22:59,275
P has two productions. P goes to plus E is predicted by plus.

189
00:22:59,725 --> 00:23:03,437
P goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of P.

190
00:23:03,887 --> 00:23:08,858
P is at the end of E goes to F P. So follow of P is follow of E.

191
00:23:09,308 --> 00:23:22,983
E is followed by dollar dollar in S goes to E dollar dollar. E is also at the end of P goes to plus E, which adds follow of P, the same set. So follow of E is dollar dollar.

192
00:23:23,433 --> 00:23:26,014
Plus against dollar dollar: disjoint.

193
00:23:26,464 --> 00:23:31,925
Q has two productions. Q goes to times F is predicted by the times sign.

194
00:23:32,375 --> 00:23:41,058
Q goes to epsilon is predicted by follow of Q. Q is at the end of F goes to int Q, so that is follow of F.

195
00:23:41,508 --> 00:23:46,799
F is followed by P in E goes to F P. Add first of P, the plus.

196
00:23:47,249 --> 00:23:59,366
P is nullable, so add follow of E, dollar dollar. F at the end of Q goes to times F adds follow of Q, the same set. So follow of F is plus and dollar dollar.

197
00:23:59,816 --> 00:24:07,752
The notes write first of P union follow of P. It gives the same set, because follow of P equals follow of E.

198
00:24:08,202 --> 00:24:15,434
Times against plus and dollar dollar: disjoint. Both pairs are disjoint, so the grammar is L L one.

199
00:24:15,884 --> 00:24:23,372
Our python check also confirms that it accepts the same strings as the ambiguous grammar, up to 8 tokens.

200
00:24:23,822 --> 00:24:31,651
And int plus int plus int parses as int plus, int plus int in brackets. The grammar is right associative.

201
00:24:32,101 --> 00:24:40,784
A short answer question from questions pre, and also in the notes. What kind of machine recognizes an L L one language?

202
00:24:41,234 --> 00:24:42,877
Pause the video and try it.

203
00:24:43,327 --> 00:24:50,217
The answer is a pushdown automaton. Figure 4.2 of the notes shows it in the L L one row.

204
00:24:50,667 --> 00:24:56,555
It is a deterministic pushdown automaton, and it runs in linear time, order n.

205
00:24:57,005 --> 00:25:04,024
A context free grammar in general needs a nondeterministic one, and order n cubed time.

206
00:25:04,474 --> 00:25:11,919
To summarize. A grammar is L L one when the predict sets of each non-terminal's productions are disjoint.

207
00:25:12,369 --> 00:25:23,143
Nullable: yes if the non-terminal derives epsilon directly, or if one of its productions has only nullable symbols. A terminal never vanishes.

208
00:25:23,593 --> 00:25:33,641
First: read the right side from the left, and go past a symbol only if it is nullable. First of epsilon is empty. Dollar dollar is a token.

209
00:25:34,091 --> 00:25:43,307
Follow: add first of what comes after A. Add follow of D when A is at the end of a production for D, or when the rest is nullable.

210
00:25:43,757 --> 00:25:55,383
Predict: first of the right side, plus follow of A if the right side can vanish. Solve the equations by iteration until nothing changes. Then compare the predict sets.
